  **Operational notes from the 2026-05-20 session:**
  - The Anvil app name is `"Argus Automated BOM"` (per uplink server
    log line `Connected to "Argus Automated BOM" as SERVER`). The
    `ANVIL_UPLINK_KEY` env var on the current Paperspace box is the
    value to copy to AWS; do NOT regenerate, that would invalidate
    the existing connection.
  - Anvil uplink is **outbound-only** (`anvil.server.connect` ->
    `wss://anvil.works/uplink`); no inbound firewall rules needed and
    no Elastic IP is technically required for the uplink itself. The
    EIP set up on 2026-05-21 AM (`52.21.216.68`) is purely an SSH
    convenience for Marco, not a technical requirement.
  - Running `DevEnv/capacity_test.py` (or any other big multiprocessing
    spawner) while the production `anvil-uplink.service` is also running
    will OOM the GPU -- 8 workers x ~3 GiB EasyOCR VRAM > 24 GiB A10G.
    Always `sudo systemctl stop anvil-uplink` before benchmarking.
  - **2026-05-20 incident**: after the capacity-test benchmark + uplink
    restart, slot-3 worker died at startup with `FileNotFoundError` on
    `multiprocessing.SemLock._rebuild`. This is a known intermittent
    Python multiprocessing race where the parent eagerly unlinks
    per-worker semaphore files from `/dev/shm` before the slowest-
    spawning worker (slot-3, since it imports last under contention)
    can attach. Production ran at 3/4 worker capacity for ~10 min
    until detected. Resolution: `sudo systemctl restart anvil-uplink`
    (single clean restart got all 4 workers up). Mitigation guidance:
    if `journalctl -u anvil-uplink` shows `Worker [slot-N] did not
    become ready within 120s`, just restart the unit -- the race is
    intermittent and a quiet system usually wins the race on retry.

